S4. The same test across the FULL Hiss sequence -- both footings, both error channels
====================================================================================================
  SHAPE, CORRECTED: the measured cutoff is NON-MONOTONIC. It peaks at z = 2.8 (22.67 km/s)
  and FALLS both ways -- to 18.22 km/s at z = 2.0 and 20.80 km/s at z = 3.4, with a weak
  secondary dip at z = 2.2 (16.89, worst error bar in the table). The retired sequence
  asserted a MONOTONIC RISE toward low z (22 -> 24 km/s from z = 2.85 to z = 2.30); that is
  the OPPOSITE sense to the measurement on the low-z side. The corpus had the SHAPE wrong,
  not merely the values. Schaye et al. 2000 Fig. 4 documents a rise toward low z at FIXED
  N_HI = 10^13.6 in simulation model L1, but Hiss's b0 is quoted at a z-DEPENDENT pivot
  (log N_HI,0 = 0.6225(1+z) + 11.1068, climbing 0.872 dex across the eight bins), so the two
  are different statistics and Schaye's direction does not license the retired numbers.
  ALSO: Hiss stops at z = 3.4, so the retired z = 3.70 point -- which used to carry the
  'exclusion dies at high z' conclusion -- has NO counterpart in the replacement data.
