====================================================================================================
S6. THE COUNTERWEIGHT -- Arnold, Puchwein & Springel 2015, stated BOTH ways
====================================================================================================
  Arnold, Puchwein & Springel 2015, MNRAS 448, 2275 (DOI 10.1093/mnras/stv146;
  arXiv:1411.2600), 'The Lyman-alpha forest in f(R) modified gravity', found: 'Even models
  with strong modifications of gravity, like |f_R0| = 1e-4, do not change the statistical
  Lyman-alpha properties by more than 10%. The column density and line width distributions
  are hardly affected at all.' Specifically flux PDF <=7% (1e-5) to <=10% (1e-4); flux P(k)
  <=5% to ~10%; matter P(k) up to ~15-17% at z=2 near k ~ 30-100 h/Mpc.
  SCOPE CORRECTIONS carried: only |f_R0| = 1e-4 and 1e-5 were simulated (NOT 1e-6), and the
  Voigt-fit line-width/column-density null is shown for 1e-5 ONLY (the 1e-4 runs used the
  smaller 15 Mpc/h box). The often-quoted ~30% temperature effect is Arnold et al. 2014 for
  gas in COLLAPSED OBJECTS, not a forest result of this paper.

  WHY IT IS A REAL COUNTERWEIGHT (this side runs the framework's way): the forest's line-
  width PDF is a velocity-sensitive statistic -- their forward model carries peculiar
  velocities and Doppler shifts explicitly -- and it came out NULL under a strong gravity
  modification. That is direct evidence that a large predicted b signature is specific to
  the POINTWISE reading rather than generic to modified dynamics. And their paper documents
  that the forest is systematics-limited at roughly the 10% level for ANY model: GR itself
  misses Kim et al. 2007 at z = 3 by more than the error bars, blamed on continuum placement
  and unmodelled low-density IGM heating.

  WHY IT DOES NOT TRANSFER AS REASSURANCE (this side runs against it): f(R) is not the same
  object. Their eqs. 9-10 give grad^2 Phi = 4 pi G (delta_rho + delta_rho_eff) with
  delta_rho_eff = delta_rho/3 - delta_R/(24 pi G), which in the fully unscreened limit caps
  the enhancement at G_eff/G = 4/3, i.e. +33.3%. A compression factor measured at a 1.33x
  force change carries no information about a ~20x response change. It modifies the Poisson
  SOURCE (a density-sourced, Yukawa-ranged, additive force) whereas MI modifies the RESPONSE,
  keyed to |a| not rho, with no Yukawa cutoff. Note also that 'screening explains their null'
  is the WEAKEST available reason: forest gas at delta ~ 0-10 is largely UNSCREENED. The null
  is driven by the +33% ceiling, the scalar's finite Compton range (their Fig. 7 shows the
  effect growing with k and toward LOWER z, away from the forest), and mean-transmission
  tuning that absorbs absolute normalisation.

  AND THE FORK THE FRAMEWORK STILL OWES, which decides whether this paper is irrelevant or
  supportive -- it must not be picked silently:
   * LOCAL / pointwise branch: y = g_pec/a0 = 4.0e-03-1.3e-02 (canonical), so
     nu = sqrt(1+1/y) = 8.9-15.8. Enormous. The f(R) paper says NOTHING about this.
   branch                                y     nu - 1
   EFE cH(z)/a0 canonical z=2        21.21      2.33%
   EFE cH(z)/a0 canonical z=3        31.94      1.55%
   EFE cH(z)/a0 alt z=2              17.57      2.81%
   EFE cH(z)/a0 alt z=3              26.46      1.87%
   EFE, alt a0(z)=a0*E(z) z=2         5.79      8.28%
   EFE, alt a0(z)=a0*E(z) z=3         5.79      8.28%
   * EFE / cosmological-field branch: nu - 1 = 1.6% to 8.3% across both
     footings and the rising variant -- SMALLER than or comparable to f(R)'s <10%, so on THIS
     branch Arnold et al.'s null transfers a fortiori and the forest says nothing against the
     framework at all.
  [OK] the two branches differ by orders: local y ~ 4e-03-1e-02 giving nu up to 16x, versus EFE y = 5.8-31.9 giving nu - 1 <= 8.3% -- so this whole test constrains ONE branch of an unclosed framework fork, and the counterweight is real on the other

====================================================================================================
S7. VERDICT
====================================================================================================
  THE POINTWISE READING IS EXCLUDED BY THE REAL DATA -- and the real data is not the data
  this script used to use. Under a pointwise application of the first-moment closure to
  diffuse baryons, with the single-absorber acceleration estimate, the framework predicts
  Lyman-alpha b-parameters above the measured Hiss et al. 2018 cutoff at
  4-26 sigma on the table's own statistical bars and 1.7-2.9 sigma against the
  3.36 km/s cutoff-fitting calibration systematic, on both a0 footings, in all
  8 redshift bins, and for every split of the non-thermal budget. Survival would need
  f_pec < 1e-01 even on the more forgiving channel.

  WHAT THE CORRECTION DID TO THE NUMBER, in both directions, because both happened:
   * SHARPER: the invented symmetric +/- 2.0 km/s bar is replaced by Hiss's real 0.33-1.37
     km/s statistical errors, which raises the statistical significance.
   * SOFTER: a calibration systematic of 3.36 km/s (2.99 on the 2sigma-rejection
     variant) now has to be carried, which Table 4 does NOT contain, and it is 4.6x the
     median statistical bar. Every significance is quoted against both.
   * SHAPE REVERSED at low z: the retired sequence's rise to 24 km/s at z = 2.30 is refuted
     by Rudie's own 18.50 +/- 0.22 km/s at the same redshift and pivot; the measured cutoff
     turns over at z = 2.8 and falls to 18.22 km/s by z = 2.0. Lower observed cutoffs at low
     z leave LESS non-thermal budget, which cuts both ways in the arithmetic and is computed
     rather than argued in S4.
   * THE 'DIES AT z = 3.7' CLAIM IS UNEVALUABLE: Hiss stops at z = 3.4. Any banked statement
     about z = 3.7 rested on a value with no published source and is withdrawn, not softened.

  WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT KILL:
   * It does NOT kill the framework's galaxy-scale phenomenology. Galaxies sit at x ~ 0.1-1
     where the amplification is 1.1-2.8x, and the RAR (0.108 dex) is untouched.
   * It does NOT kill the framework. It kills ONE READING of it -- the pointwise one -- and
     that reading was already flagged as suspect from a completely independent direction:
     mi_channelA_friedmann_2026 found K ~ sqrt(z) at the origin with h -> 0, so a pointwise
     linear-response treatment has no small expansion parameter exactly where it is being
     applied. Two independent arguments converge on the same conclusion.
   * It DOES convert the missing regulator from optional to MANDATORY, with a number: any
     viable MI cosmology must suppress the diffuse-baryon response by a factor >~ 2
     relative to the pointwise reading, or explain why forest gas is not in the deep regime.

  THE LEGITIMATE ESCAPES, and each is a real research question rather than a dodge:
   1. THE EFE / TOTAL-ACCELERATION ARGUMENT, which is the same one that fixed my channel-A
      error, and which S6 now shows is the whole ballgame: on the EFE branch the framework
      predicts nu - 1 = 1.6-8.3%, BELOW f(R)'s <10%, and Arnold et al. 2015's
      null transfers a fortiori. Deciding the branch is the single highest-value next step.
   2. THE x-CONVENTION FORK (S5). a_N for forest gas spans 2.6 dex on convention alone
      inside Aguirre et al.'s own paper. The exclusion survives all of it, but the SIZE of
      the number is convention-owned and no precise sigma should be quoted bare.
   3. PRESSURE SUPPORT. Forest gas is photoionized and pressure-supported, so its velocity
      structure may be thermally rather than gravitationally set. The S3 scan tests this and
      finds it insufficient by orders of magnitude -- but a proper treatment needs sims.
   4. THE LCDM-HYDRO CALIBRATION. The b budget decomposition is calibrated on standard-
      gravity simulations, and Arnold et al. document that the forest is systematics-limited
      at the ~10% level for everyone. A self-consistent MI hydro run is still owed.
   5. THE NON-ANALYTICITY. If the pointwise linear response is simply invalid at small x,
      this test constrains nothing about the true theory. That is the most likely resolution
      and also the least satisfying, because it means the framework currently has NO
      calculable prediction in the diffuse sector at all.

  NET: a genuine, sharp, adverse observational result against the only calculable version of
  the framework's diffuse-sector prediction -- now resting on a sourced published table
  instead of four numbers that could not be found in any paper. Reported as found. The
  framework's galaxy-scale content is untouched; its cosmological-sector content is
  demonstrably incomplete rather than merely unbuilt.
